WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.450 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I've always wondered why in a nation so  rich are some people so poor to me it's  always seemed so old-fashioned  like governments that don't resemble a  democracy or voting rights that don't  include women or like these wool  cardigans things that just shouldn't  exist  growing up I often ask myself this  question why does poverty still exist as  a daycare provider my mother made enough  money for us to live just above the  poverty line  despite providing a critical service to  society she stressed every day about  whether we have enough to pay for rent  or buy groceries she'd work more and  more hours just to escape being poor as  if it were a privilege she had to earn  to me that just didn't seem right the  truth about poverty and inequality in  the u.s. is far worse than my own  childhood might have indicated in the  United States one out of every five  children live in absolute poverty a  poverty rate higher than what prevails  in virtually all other rich nations  inequality in the u.s. is the highest  it's been since 1928  right before the Great Depression over  the past 35 years  the real average income of the top point  zero one percent has grown by three  hundred and twenty-two percent while  that of the bottom 90% has largely  remained stagnant in the American dream  of working hard and finding a way out of  poverty no longer applies increasingly  the greatest predictor of your future  success is not your education or your  perseverance  but your parents income and your race to  make matters worse economists now say  that inequality is so large that it's  had a negative impact on an overall  economic growth something we can all  care about people are poor absolutely  relatively and permanently and all at a  cost to our nation's growth so what can  we do about two years ago after reading  everything I could find to understand  poverty and inequality better and what  we can do to address it I stumbled  across a book it's called utopia for  realist by Rutger Bregman in the book he  chronicles an idea one so simple I  thought it couldn't possibly work it's  an idea that dates back 500 years to  Thomas More in his own book called  utopia some refer to it as the citizens  dividend others call it a basic income  so what is it it's an unconditional  monthly cash grant enough to pay for  your basic needs food shelter education  that's it it's not a favor but a right  as divisive as it might seem its action  ideas supported by individuals across  the political spectrum from civil rights  activists dr. Martin Luther King jr. who  praised it for its fairness to  free-market economist Milton Friedman  who appreciated how it instilled freedom  of choice now when I tell people we  should just give free money to everyone  they usually say you socialist and then  they respond with one of three concerns  people will spend it on drugs and  alcohol people will stop working we  can't afford to pay for it so today  I hope to share what I learned about  each of these three concerns and  hopefully show that a basic income is  more than just an idea it's a proven  idea number one people will spend it on  drugs and alcohol the underlying  assumption with this concern stated  bluntly is that there are somehow  something wrong with poor people poor  people make poor decisions like doing  drugs and alcohol and that's probably  why the poor to begin with because of  this mindset the approach has always  been the same if we could just teach  them if we could teach them how to live  life with our values in our culture if  they would only just listen what this  mindset fails to recognize are the  drastic effects of living in poverty  some poor people make poor decisions  because they're poor however if you  change the context you can change the  actions too World Bank researchers found  that in nineteen different cash transfer  programs across ten different nations  spending on alcohol and cigarettes went  down given hope and a brighter future  individuals stopped using temptation  goods as a way to cope with their  current situation instead the extra cash  went to pay for their kids school fees  to buy medicine to repair their homes to  invest in their small businesses to  further grow their wealth as Bregman  concluded poverty is not the result of  poor decision-making or lack of moral  character poverty is a lack of cash  number two people will stop working in  the 1970s there's a basic income  experiment in a small town in Canada  every family received enough money to  ensure they were lift above the poverty  line and for four years all went well  until a new Canadian government was  voted into power that immediately ended  the  Behrman 25 years later a Canadian  professor found and analyzed these  records and the results were clear  school performance went up while mental  health complaints domestic violence  incidents and hospitalization rate all  went down and no one quit their jobs the  only ones who worked a little less were  new mothers caring for their child and  students who decided to stay in school  longer which brings me to the third and  final concern even if this is all true  we can't afford to do it actually we  can't afford not to do it  poverty is extremely expensive the cost  of child poverty in the u.s. is  estimated at five hundred billion  dollars in terms of higher health care  costs higher dropout rates and more  crime this is an incredible waste of  human potential a basic income could be  financed with what's known as a negative  income tax credit which essentially  means that your income is topped up as  soon as you fall below the poverty line  under this scenario according to  economists best estimates for cost of  one hundred and seventy five billion  dollars less than one percent of GDP  less than four percent of our nation's  annual budget we could actually  eradicate poverty as bregman No  basic income is like venture capital for  the people we invest now and after 10 to  20 years they'll pay for itself in terms  of lower healthcare costs less crime and  more productive citizens putting the  money to work and stimulating our  economy according to current estimates a  basic income started today would result  in an increase of GDP of 13 percent by  2025 or 2.5 trillion dollars so when  given cash people spend less on drugs  and alcohol work just as much and we  actually save and generate money well if  all this is so obviously true why  haven't we done it already the truth is  we tried in the late 1960s we piloted a  basic income for close to nine thousand  individuals across seven different  states and by every measure as you might  have guessed by now the pilot was a  success except for one researchers found  that for those families receiving a  basic income divorce rates increased by  50% now this was something politicians  of the time just cannot get behind a  basic income had apparently given women  too much independence however just ten  years later in a reanalysis of the data  researchers found that this was a  statistical error but by that time the  political moment had passed now we are  seeing a reemergence of the basic income  Silicon Valley has taken up the idea  based on fear that robots will one day  take our jobs however while the future  is uncertain the present is clear  poverty exists where and when it  shouldn't we need to rid ourselves of  the bye seas we have about the poor we  need to acknowledge the wealth  we have to address the problem and the  potential that we will unlock if we do  so we need to redefine our social  contract to say that no one will live in  poverty but to do this most of all we  need the will and that will must be  founded on the belief that a life  without poverty should not be a  privilege if to earn but a right that we  all deserve  [Applause]  [Music]

